<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xml:lang="en"><generator uri="https://jekyllrb.com/" version="4.4.1">Jekyll</generator><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/feed.xml" rel="self" type="application/atom+xml"/><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" hreflang="en"/><updated>2026-10-18T20:35:07-04:00</updated><id>https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/feed.xml</id><title type="html">blank</title><subtitle>Chonghe Jiang is a PhD student at MIT IDSS studying optimization, reinforcement learning, and LLM post-training.</subtitle><entry><title type="html">Revisiting Recent Trends in Self-Improving Agents for Autoresearch</title><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-autoresearch/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Revisiting Recent Trends in Self-Improving Agents for Autoresearch"/><published>2026-10-15T00:00:00-04:00</published><updated>2026-10-15T00:00:00-04:00</updated><id>https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/autoresearch-as-optimization</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-autoresearch/"><![CDATA[<h2 id="methods">Methods</h2> <figure class="ar-figure ar-map ar-evolution" id="ar-map" aria-label="Methods in the self-improvement loop"> <div class="ar-map-controls ar-grouped-controls" hidden="" role="group" aria-label="Papers grouped by contribution"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-components="all" data-title="Shared loop" data-description="Feedback updates the search state; marked methods also train model parameters." data-method="Overview">Overview</button> <div class="ar-method-group" role="group" aria-labelledby="ar-group-state"><span class="ar-group-label" id="ar-group-state">Context management</span><div class="ar-group-papers"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="propose memory" data-title="Fork-and-Flush" data-description="Fork the workspace into fresh-context continuations, then continue the best branch." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07447" data-method="Fork-and-Flush">Fork-and-Flush</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent memory" data-title="Scaling Discovery Requires Forgetting" data-description="Restart the team from the best candidate after clearing sessions and the other candidate history." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20563" data-method="Forgetting">Forgetting</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent propose memory" data-title="Stateless Language Agents" data-description="The harness retains evidence and rebuilds each context; an Advisor assigns experiments to Workers." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07625" data-method="SLA">SLA</button> </div></div> <div class="ar-method-group" role="group" aria-labelledby="ar-group-decisions"><span class="ar-group-label" id="ar-group-decisions">Search decisions</span><div class="ar-group-papers"> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent" data-title="Contrastive Concept-Tree Search" data-description="Use concepts associated with strong and weak programs to guide parent selection and edits." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03132" data-method="CCTS">CCTS</button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent" data-title="MIRA-AC" data-description="Learn research decisions from their downstream returns while keeping the executor fixed." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02525" data-training-label="Trained decision policy · fixed executor" data-method="MIRA-AC">MIRA-AC <span class="ar-training-dot" role="img" aria-label="Updates model parameters" title="Updates model parameters"></span></button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent propose evaluate" data-title="IdeaScientist" data-description="GRPO trains gap finding, innovation and report writing; a fixed orchestrator coordinates research proposal generation." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04074" data-training-label="Trained ideation roles · fixed orchestrator" data-method="IdeaScientist">IdeaScientist <span class="ar-training-dot" role="img" aria-label="Updates model parameters" title="Updates model parameters"></span></button> <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-components="parent propose" data-method="Guidance-TTT" data-title="Evolving in Thought Space" data-description="Train strategic guidance at test time; a frozen executor implements it." data-training-label="Test-time training · frozen executor" data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06269">Evolving in Thought Space <span class="ar-training-dot" role="img" aria-label="Updates model parameters" title="Updates model parameters"></span></button> </div></div> </div> <div class="ar-evo-canvas"> <svg class="ar-evo-edges ar-evo-wide" viewBox="0 0 720 322" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><defs><marker id="ar-arrow-wide" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar-arrow-wide)"><path d="M336 72H382"/><path d="M552 144V176"/><path d="M384 250H338"/><path d="M168 178V146"/></g></svg> <svg class="ar-evo-edges ar-evo-narrow" viewBox="0 0 340 406" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><defs><marker id="ar-arrow-narrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0L10 5L0 10Z" fill="currentColor"/></marker></defs><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar-arrow-narrow)"><path d="M162 95H176"/><path d="M259 190V214"/><path d="M178 311H164"/><path d="M81 216V192"/></g></svg> <ol class="ar-evo-nodes"> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-parent" data-component="parent"><span class="ar-number">01</span><strong>Select &amp; adapt</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Choose a parent and use feedback to steer the next attempt.</p></li> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-propose" data-component="propose"><span class="ar-number">02</span><strong>Generate</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Revise a candidate or carry out an investigation.</p></li> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-evaluate" data-component="evaluate"><span class="ar-number">03</span><strong>Evaluate</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Measure quality and correctness.</p></li> <li class="ar-evo-node ar-evo-memory" data-component="memory"><span class="ar-number">04</span><strong>Select &amp; remember</strong><p class="ar-node-copy">Retain useful candidates and evidence for the next round.</p></li> </ol> </div> <div class="ar-map-detail" hidden="" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><span class="ar-training-label">Search-state updates; policy training is optional</span><strong>Shared loop</strong><p>Feedback updates the search state; marked methods also train model parameters.</p><a hidden="">Read the paper ↗</a></div> <figcaption>Select a paper to see its changes. <span class="ar-training-key"><span class="ar-training-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> Updates model parameters</span></figcaption> </figure> <h2 id="takeaways">Takeaways</h2> <p>These papers highlight two ways to improve research agents: refresh their context, or train the roles that decide what to investigate next.</p> <figure class="ar-takeaway" id="ar-takeaways" aria-label="Two perspectives on research agents"> <div class="ar-takeaway-modes" hidden="" role="tablist" aria-label="Training approach"> <button type="button" id="ar-mode-context" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="ar-context-takeaway" tabindex="0">Training-free</button> <button type="button" id="ar-mode-learning" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="ar-learning-takeaway" tabindex="-1">With training</button> </div> <div class="ar-takeaway-panel" id="ar-context-takeaway"> <header class="ar-takeaway-heading"><h3 id="ar-context-title">Refresh context</h3><p>Carry useful results forward while giving the next attempt a fresh context.</p></header> <div class="ar-takeaway-switch" hidden="" role="group" aria-label="Compare context management methods"> <button type="button" data-variant="fork" aria-pressed="true">Fork-and-Flush</button> <button type="button" data-variant="forget" aria-pressed="false">Forgetting</button> <button type="button" data-variant="sla" aria-pressed="false">SLA</button> </div> <div class="ar-takeaway-diagram" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"> <div class="ar-takeaway-flow"> <div class="ar-takeaway-node"><span class="ar-takeaway-role">Retain</span><strong data-field="first">Accumulated workspace</strong><p data-field="firstCopy">Artifacts from the search so far.</p></div> <span class="ar-takeaway-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <div class="ar-takeaway-node ar-takeaway-focus"><span class="ar-takeaway-role">Rebuild</span><strong data-field="second">Fresh contexts</strong><p data-field="secondCopy">Each branch starts a new conversation.</p><span class="ar-reset-note" data-field="reset">Reset chat history</span></div> <span class="ar-takeaway-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <div class="ar-takeaway-node"><span class="ar-takeaway-role">Explore</span><strong data-field="third">Parallel attempts</strong><p data-field="thirdCopy">Run each branch for a fixed horizon.</p></div> </div> <div class="ar-takeaway-return"><span data-field="feedback">Continue from the best branch</span></div> </div> <p class="ar-takeaway-caption"><span data-field="note">The workspace survives the reset; the winning branch supplies the next starting point.</span> <a data-source="" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07447">Read the paper ↗</a></p> </div> <div class="ar-takeaway-panel" id="ar-learning-takeaway"> <header class="ar-takeaway-heading"><h3 id="ar-learning-title">Train the research decisions.</h3><p>Give high-level decisions a dedicated learning role, with feedback on the work they produce.</p></header> <div class="ar-takeaway-switch" hidden="" role="group" aria-label="Compare trainable research roles"> <button type="button" data-variant="mira" aria-pressed="false">MIRA-AC</button> <button type="button" data-variant="idea" aria-pressed="false">IdeaScientist</button> <button type="button" data-variant="guidance" aria-pressed="true">Evolving in Thought Space</button> </div> <div class="ar-takeaway-diagram" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"> <div class="ar-takeaway-flow"> <div class="ar-takeaway-node ar-takeaway-focus"><span class="ar-takeaway-role">Learn</span><strong data-field="first">Guidance model θ</strong><p data-field="firstCopy">Propose strategic edits to a parent solution.</p></div> <span class="ar-takeaway-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <div class="ar-takeaway-node"><span class="ar-takeaway-role" data-field="middleRole">Execute</span><strong data-field="second">Frozen executor</strong><p data-field="secondCopy">Implement the edits as a complete program.</p></div> <span class="ar-takeaway-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> <div class="ar-takeaway-node"><span class="ar-takeaway-role">Assess</span><strong data-field="third">Task verifier</strong><p data-field="thirdCopy">Run the program and measure its quality.</p></div> </div> <div class="ar-takeaway-return"><span data-field="feedback">Verifier reward updates guidance θ</span></div> </div> <p class="ar-takeaway-caption"><span data-field="note">Learning happens on the current task. Only the guidance model changes; the executor stays frozen.</span> <a data-source="" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06269">Read the paper ↗</a></p> </div> </figure> <h2 id="next-step">What’s the Next Step?</h2> <p>I see evaluation time as a major constraint on self-improving research agents. A promising idea still needs to be tested, and those tests can dominate the research budget. In <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.17184#A4.SS1">DuoDiscover’s vLLM study</a>, one full evaluation takes about 85 minutes. This points to two directions.</p> <h3 id="use-fewer-evaluations">Use fewer evaluations</h3> <p>How can we find a better solution with fewer calls to the evaluator? Training-free methods can use past results to select more informative experiments and avoid repeating failed attempts. With training, a research policy can learn which directions are worth pursuing. Both aim for <strong>sample-efficient search</strong>: reaching a stronger verified result within a fixed evaluation budget.</p> <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11794">Memento 3</a> offers a training-free example. The agent turns interaction feedback into a revisable handbook of environment rules, then compiles those rules into an executable world model. It checks the model against past observations and uses it to plan future actions. The LLM stays frozen; the handbook and simulator improve as evidence accumulates. This illustrates an algorithmic route to fewer real interactions: use what the agent has learned to guide its next attempt.</p> <h3 id="make-evaluations-cheaper">Make evaluations cheaper</h3> <p>Can a cheap approximation tell us which candidates deserve a full evaluation? Smaller test suites, shorter runs or surrogate models could make it possible to explore more candidates with the same resources.</p> <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17184">DuoDiscover</a> offers an example: agents develop optimization targets together with cheap, tailored proxy evaluators. They search using these proxies, then confirm improvements with the full evaluator. Fidelity tests also check whether proxy rankings still agree with full evaluations as the search progresses.</p> <p>Working on self-improving agents, I’m always interested in the models people choose and the tasks they test them on. Some familiar tasks, such as Erdős problems and packing benchmarks, already seem close to saturation. I’ve collected the models and tasks from these papers below as a reference for planning new experiments.</p> <ul> <li><a href="/blog/2026/revisiting-autoresearch/models/">Models</a> — model choices, agent setups and evaluation budgets.</li> <li><a href="/blog/2026/revisiting-autoresearch/tasks/">Tasks</a> — benchmarks, evaluation time, resources and recent progress.</li> </ul>]]></content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html"><![CDATA[LLM agents work on one problem, using evaluation feedback to search for the best solution within a fixed evaluation budget.]]></summary></entry><entry><title type="html">Revisiting PPO for LLM Post-Training</title><link href="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo/" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="Revisiting PPO for LLM Post-Training"/><published>2026-10-14T00:00:00-04:00</published><updated>2026-10-14T00:00:00-04:00</updated><id>https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo</id><content type="html" xml:base="https://chonghe-jiang.github.io/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo/"><![CDATA[<p>This post reviews <strong>PPO and related policy-gradient methods for large language models</strong>.</p> <p><span id="ppo-review"></span> Need a refresher on PPO? <a href="/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo/foundations/">Start with the basics</a>.</p> <h2 id="literature">Review of Recent Work</h2> <figure class="ppo-map" id="ppo-paper-map" aria-labelledby="ppo-map-title"> <div class="ppo-map-heading"><span class="note-label">From rollout to update</span><h3 id="ppo-map-title">Where recent papers change PPO</h3></div> <ol class="ppo-stages"> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="1"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">01</span></div><strong>Rollouts</strong><p>Generate trajectories</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" data-paper="sao"><span class="ppo-paper-name">SAO</span></a><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/" data-paper="flash"><span class="ppo-paper-name">FlashREINFORCE</span></a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="2"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">02</span></div><strong>Reward &amp; value</strong><p>Evaluate outcomes and prefixes</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19295" data-paper="tempo"><span class="ppo-paper-name">TEMPO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39247" data-paper="ac2"><span class="ppo-paper-name">AC2</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23566" data-paper="bpco"><span class="ppo-paper-name">BPCO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15840" data-paper="lvrl"><span class="ppo-paper-name">LVRL</span></a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="3"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">03</span></div><strong>Advantages</strong><p>Assign credit relative to a baseline</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" data-paper="sao"><span class="ppo-paper-name">SAO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39247" data-paper="ac2"><span class="ppo-paper-name">AC2</span></a><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/" data-paper="flash"><span class="ppo-paper-name">FlashREINFORCE</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23566" data-paper="bpco"><span class="ppo-paper-name">BPCO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15840" data-paper="lvrl"><span class="ppo-paper-name">LVRL</span></a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="4"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">04</span></div><strong>Policy objective</strong><p>Correct probabilities and limit updates</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20807" data-paper="centering"><span class="ppo-paper-name">Score Centering</span></a><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md" data-paper="decoupled"><span class="ppo-paper-name">Decoupled PPO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23566" data-paper="bpco"><span class="ppo-paper-name">BPCO</span></a></div></li> <li class="ppo-stage" data-stage="5"><div class="ppo-stage-top"><span class="ppo-stage-number">05</span></div><strong>Updates</strong><p>Optimize actor and critic</p><div class="ppo-stage-papers"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36802" data-paper="easy"><span class="ppo-paper-name">EasyPPO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" data-paper="sao"><span class="ppo-paper-name">SAO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19295" data-paper="tempo"><span class="ppo-paper-name">TEMPO</span></a><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md" data-paper="decoupled"><span class="ppo-paper-name">Decoupled PPO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23566" data-paper="bpco"><span class="ppo-paper-name">BPCO</span></a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20597" data-paper="cope"><span class="ppo-paper-name">CoPE</span></a></div></li> </ol> <div class="ppo-loop"><span aria-hidden="true">↶</span> Updated policy and value model feed the next round</div> <div class="ppo-map-interaction" hidden=""> <div class="ppo-paper-select" role="group" aria-label="Highlight a paper's contributions"><button type="button" data-paper="all" aria-pressed="true">All papers</button> <button type="button" data-paper="sao" data-stages="1 3 5" data-detail="Single-rollout asynchronous sampling, a learned value baseline, and strict double-sided token clipping." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.07508" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">SAO</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="easy" data-stages="5" data-detail="Stabilize critic training with actor-only overlong filtering, noise-normalized regression, and smaller critic minibatches." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.36802" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">EasyPPO</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="tempo" data-stages="2 5" data-detail="Use critic feedback for policy refinement on unlabeled questions, then recalibrate the critic on labeled data." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.19295" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">TEMPO</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="ac2" data-stages="2 3" data-detail="Use generative critic estimates for action-chunk advantages and updates, gated by local readiness." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.39247" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">AC2</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="flash" data-stages="1 3" data-detail="Single-rollout asynchronous learning with batch centering replaces the learned value baseline. This is a critic-free alternative to PPO." data-url="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">FlashREINFORCE</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="centering" data-stages="4" data-detail="Apply an additive correction for gradient drift from training–inference mismatch. Score here means the log-probability gradient." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20807" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">Score Centering</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="decoupled" data-stages="4 5" data-detail="Separate behavior-policy importance correction from clipping around a recent proximal policy." data-url="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">Decoupled PPO</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="bpco" data-stages="2 3 4 5" data-detail="Bounded value predictions, Monte Carlo targets, raw advantages, length-adaptive GAE, and Divergence PPO clipping." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23566" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">BPCO</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="lvrl" data-stages="2 3" data-detail="A frozen LLM assigns step credit through anchored hindsight value differences, conditioned on group context." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15840" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">LVRL</span></button> <button type="button" data-paper="cope" data-stages="5" data-detail="Add successful-trajectory action forecasting to GRPO using the policy backbone; no learned value critic." data-url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20597" aria-pressed="false"><span class="ppo-paper-name">CoPE</span></button> </div> <div class="ppo-paper-detail" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><span class="ppo-detail-name">Explore the loop</span><p>Select a paper to highlight the stages it changes.</p><a hidden="">Read the paper ↗</a></div> </div> <figcaption>Select a paper to locate its changes. FlashREINFORCE and CoPE use critic-free updates; LVRL uses a frozen critic.</figcaption> </figure> <p><strong>Agent:</strong> SAO, LVRL, CoPE, EasyPPO, FlashREINFORCE.</p> <p><strong>LLM:</strong> TEMPO, AC2, Score Centering, Decoupled PPO¹, BPCO, EasyPPO, FlashREINFORCE.</p> <h3 id="where-the-papers-intervene">Where the papers intervene</h3> <p>Reward centering changes the advantage. Score centering changes the log-probability gradient; below, $w$ denotes its importance weight. A frozen LLM can replace learned value estimates, while an auxiliary prediction loss can add supervision without estimating values at all.</p> <div class="table-scroll ppo-literature-table" role="region" aria-label="Papers mapped to PPO components" tabindex="0"> <table> <thead> <tr> <th style="text-align: left">Work</th> <th style="text-align: left">RL setting</th> <th style="text-align: left">Component</th> <th style="text-align: left">What changes</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2607.07508v1">SAO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Agent</td> <td style="text-align: left">Rollouts · GAE · updates</td> <td style="text-align: left">One rollout per prompt, asynchronous collection, skip-observation GAE, strict token masking, and more frequent critic updates.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/">FlashREINFORCE</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Both</td> <td style="text-align: left">Rollouts · baseline</td> <td style="text-align: left">Uses $R_i-\bar R_{\mathrm{batch}}$, sequence admission and per-trajectory averaging. No learned critic or $L_V$.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.19295v1">TEMPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">LLM</td> <td style="text-align: left">Value feedback</td> <td style="text-align: left">Uses $V(y_{\mathrm{complete}})-V(s_t)$ on unlabeled questions, with periodic critic recalibration on labeled data.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.39247v2">AC2</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">LLM</td> <td style="text-align: left">Chunk values · advantages</td> <td style="text-align: left">Uses $v_i-\bar v$ and critic target $\bar v$. For critic-ready problems, unfinished chunks receive $v_i=V(s\cdot c_i)$.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.20807v1">Score Centering</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">LLM</td> <td style="text-align: left">Actor gradient</td> <td style="text-align: left">Centers the weighted score: $w\psi_\theta-c(s)$, with $\psi_\theta=\nabla\log\pi_\theta$ and $c(s)=\mathbb E_{a\sim\mu}[w\psi_\theta]$. Addresses drift from training–inference mismatch.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/main/docs/en/best_practices/algo_perf.md">Decoupled PPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">LLM¹</td> <td style="text-align: left">Probability ratios</td> <td style="text-align: left">Separates behavior correction $\nu/\mu$ from clipping around $\pi_\theta/\nu$, where $\nu$ is a recent proximal policy.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.36802v1">EasyPPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Both</td> <td style="text-align: left">Critic weighting · updates</td> <td style="text-align: left">Uses $\omega_x\propto1/\max(\hat\sigma_x,\varepsilon)$, retains truncated samples for the critic, filters them for the actor, and uses smaller critic minibatches.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2608.23566v2">BPCO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">LLM</td> <td style="text-align: left">Critic · GAE · clipping</td> <td style="text-align: left">Bounds values to the reward range, uses $Y_t=R$, preserves raw advantages, and adapts $\lambda_\pi$ to length. Optional privileged critic inputs; Divergence PPO clipping.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.15840v1">LVRL</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Agent</td> <td style="text-align: left">Value estimates · step credit</td> <td style="text-align: left">A frozen LLM uses trajectory and group context to estimate values. Anchored value differences give action-level advantages; no critic optimization.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.20597v2">CoPE</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Agent</td> <td style="text-align: left">Auxiliary policy loss</td> <td style="text-align: left">Adds successful-trajectory action forecasting to GRPO on a shared backbone. It changes supervision, without adding a value critic.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div> <p>¹ Decoupled PPO refers to the linked GSM8K example. AC2’s chunks divide a proof into segments, without external interaction.</p> <h3 id="bpco-change-what-the-critic-learns">BPCO: change what the critic learns</h3> <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.23566">BPCO</a> separates the actor’s bootstrapping from the critic’s target: $\lambda_V=1$ keeps the target tied to the observed reward, while $\lambda_\pi(L)=1-1/(\alpha L)$ adapts credit propagation to response length. Its bounded head prevents impossible return predictions. An optional reference answer or rubric helps the critic during training without entering the policy’s prompt. Its <strong>Divergence PPO</strong> uses a token-probability-dependent clipping range; this is distinct from <strong>Decoupled PPO</strong> above.</p> <h3 id="lvrl-redistribute-the-outcome-across-actions">LVRL: redistribute the outcome across actions</h3> <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15840">LLM Value Functions for Credit Assignment in Agentic RL</a> uses a frozen LLM to read completed trajectories, with other rollouts providing policy context. At the environment-action level, it anchors $\tilde V_0=\bar R_G$ and $\tilde V_T=R$, then computes</p> \[\bar A_t=\tilde V_{t+1}-\tilde V_t, \qquad \sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\bar A_t=R-\bar R_G.\] <p>It divides these differences by their group-wide step standard deviation; every token in an action shares that action’s advantage. The unnormalized sum is fixed by outcomes, but the LLM determines where credit goes. These are hindsight estimates, not an ordinary prefix-only value function. Anchoring preserves the total signal; it does not guarantee correct local credit.</p> <h3 id="cope-learn-more-from-successful-trajectories">CoPE: learn more from successful trajectories</h3> <p><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20597">CoPE</a> keeps GRPO and adds a forecasting loss. From history $h_t$, the shared backbone predicts the next $K+1$ actions recorded in a successful rollout:</p> \[L_{\mathrm{AF}} =-\mathbb E_{\tau\mid\mathrm{success}} \left[\sum_t\log p_\theta(a_{t:t+K}\mid h_t)\right].\] <p>Training minimizes $L_{\mathrm{GRPO}}+\eta L_{\mathrm{AF}}$, averaging the forecasting term over successful rollouts within each group. A forecasting instruction distinguishes $p_\theta$ from the acting distribution, even though they share parameters. At inference, the agent still acts one step at a time. The added signal teaches which action sequences followed successful histories, rather than how much scalar value each history had.</p> <h2 id="models">Models &amp; Tasks</h2> <p>The experiments range from answer-only reasoning to agents that use tools over many turns. The table separates the model being trained from models that supply values or grades.</p> <div class="ar-study-table-wrap ppo-model-table" role="region" aria-label="LLM models, critics and tasks by method" tabindex="0"> <table> <thead> <tr> <th style="text-align: left">Method</th> <th style="text-align: left">Trained models</th> <th style="text-align: left">Critic / judge</th> <th style="text-align: left">Tasks</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2607.07508v1#S4.SS1">SAO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen3-30B-A3B-Thinking-2507; TIR-finetuned initialization for math.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Separate value model initialized from the policy checkpoint.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#math">Math with Python</a>; <a href="tasks/#coding">SWE-bench Verified</a> with OpenHands.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://yifanzhang-pro.github.io/FlashREINFORCE/">FlashREINFORCE</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B; Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; Qwen3-30B-A3B.</td> <td style="text-align: left">No critic.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#math">Math and Python tool use</a>; <a href="tasks/#agents">ALFWorld</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.36802v1#S5">EasyPPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen3.5-9B-Base for math/search; Qwen3.5-9B after SFT for coding.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Learned token-value critic. DeepSeek-V3.1 supplies coding SFT trajectories.</td> <td style="text-align: left">DAPO-Math-17K → AIME24; <a href="tasks/#coding">FrontierSmith → FrontierCS</a>; <a href="tasks/#agents">Search-R1</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2604.19295v1#S4.SS1">TEMPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen3-8B/14B-Base; OLMO3-7B-Base, after PPO initialization.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Learned critic; GPT-OSS-120B judges general-domain answers.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#math">Math and general reasoning</a>, with unlabeled adaptation sets and separate held-out sets.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.39247v2#S4">AC2</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Actor and generative critic share weights; DeepSeek-V4-Flash grades proofs.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#proofs">FineProofs-RL → IMO-ProofBench</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.20807v1#S5.SS1">Score Centering</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen3-0.6B-Instruct; Qwen3-30B-A3B-Base.</td> <td style="text-align: left">No learned critic in the controlled comparison; group-centered REINFORCE.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#math">Countdown and INTELLECT-2 math</a>, under controlled sampler–trainer mismatch.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://github.com/areal-project/AReaL/blob/bb13e3bdb9a865608eafa017a70a790124c3d3bb/examples/math/gsm8k_ppo.yaml">Decoupled PPO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct in the linked AReaL example.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Separate critic initialized from the same checkpoint.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#math">GSM8K</a>. This row describes a released configuration, not a separate paper experiment.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2608.23566v2#S4">BPCO</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B; Qwen3-30B-A3B Base/non-Base; Qwen3-4B-Base for rubrics.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Learned critic; optional answer/rubric inputs. Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 is the rubric judge.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#math">Math</a>: DeepScaleR or DAPO-Math-17K → AIME25; <a href="tasks/#rubrics">OpenRubrics</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.15840v1#S4.SS1">LVRL</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen3-4B/8B for AppWorld and BabyAI; Qwen3-8B/32B for repository tasks.</td> <td style="text-align: left">Frozen Claude Opus 4.6 value critic. LVOPD uses Qwen3-235B-A22B-Thinking-2507-FP8 as teacher and critic.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#agents">AppWorld, BabyAI</a>; <a href="tasks/#coding">R2E-Gym → SWE-bench Verified</a>.</td> </tr> <tr> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.20597v2#S6">CoPE</a></td> <td style="text-align: left">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct; Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct for AppWorld.</td> <td style="text-align: left">No value critic; acting and forecasting share one backbone.</td> <td style="text-align: left"><a href="tasks/#agents">ALFWorld, ScienceWorld, WebShop, τ²-bench and AppWorld</a>.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </div> <p><a class="essay-read" href="/blog/2026/revisiting-ppo/tasks/">See details on the tasks <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>]]></content><author><name></name></author><summary type="html"><![CDATA[PPO and related policy-gradient methods for LLM post-training.]]></summary></entry></feed>